  "id": "DrugBank_DB06820 - MONDO_1461",
  "verbalization": "Sulconazole, a broad-spectrum antifungal agent, is linked to Tinea Corporis through a series of indications that collectively suggest a potential mechanism of action. The evidence shows that Sulconazole is indicated for Dermatophytosis of the groin and perianal area, which is a fungal infection caused by dermatophytes. This condition is also indicated for Econazole and Oxiconazole, both of which are antifungal agents with similar mechanisms of action. Naftifine, another antifungal, is also indicated for Tinea Corporis, further reinforcing the connection between antifungal agents and the condition. The shared indication for Tinea Corporis across these different antifungals suggests a possible overlap in their therapeutic effects, potentially due to shared mechanisms of action or similar antifungal profiles. The evidence supports a relationship between Sulconazole and Tinea Corporis through the indirect pathway of antifungal indications, highlighting the role of antifungal agents in the treatment of fungal infections.",
